IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `coa-core`

-- coa2-ch1-prohibited-names.l4: Part 5 (A company's name), Chapter 1 (General requirements), ss 53-57C,
-- lines 564-638 of ukpga-2006-46.txt.  LAYER 1: the law, objectively; no point of view.
--
-- SOURCE. A plain-text rendering of the Act from legislation.gov.uk made by a converter script; it is not the
-- official text and nothing here is "verified against the official text".  Quote lines are checked by srcrefs.py.
--
-- HOW TO READ AN ANSWER (the rule of 2026-10-29, assumed, not ruled by a lawyer; see NOTES.md).
-- Every decision answers MAYBE BOOLEAN.  A prohibition ("must not be registered ... if X") is asked as the permission
-- question "may the company be registered by the name?" (the keel's s 5(1) shape):
--   JUST FALSE  the text says no in its own words (the prohibition bites).
--   JUST TRUE   the text itself says yes ("does not prevent ...").
--   NOTHING     the text does not decide: the prohibition names only its own cases, so outside them it is silent.
-- Whether the Secretary of State holds an opinion is a judgment the Act hands to the Secretary of State: it is a
-- plain input (TRUE: the Secretary of State holds that opinion), never guessed.
-- Every name that is not in coa-core.l4 begins with the section label it implements.

-- =========================================================================================================
-- Prohibited names (ss 53, 53A)
-- =========================================================================================================

-- src:567 | 53 Prohibited names
-- src:568 | A company must not be registered under this Act by a name if, in the opinion of the Secretary of State—
-- src:569 | (a) its use by the company would constitute an offence, or
-- src:570 | (b) it is offensive.
@ref s 53 Prohibited names (src:567-570)
GIVEN `in the opinion of the Secretary of State its use by the company would constitute an offence` IS A BOOLEAN
      `in the opinion of the Secretary of State it is offensive` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 53 the company may be registered by the name` MEANS
    IF       `in the opinion of the Secretary of State its use by the company would constitute an offence`
         OR  `in the opinion of the Secretary of State it is offensive`
    THEN JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "A company must not be registered under this Act by a name if, in the opinion of the Secretary of State—"
    ELSE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the prohibition names only the cases in (a) and (b)

-- src:571 | 53A Names for criminal purposes
-- src:572 | A company must not be registered under this Act by a name if, in the opinion of the Secretary of State, the registration of the company by that name is intended to facilitate—
-- src:573 | (a) the commission of an offence involving dishonesty or deception, or
-- src:574 | (b) the carrying out of conduct that, if carried out in any part of the United Kingdom, would amount to such an offence.
@ref s 53A Names for criminal purposes (src:571-574)
GIVEN `in the opinion of the Secretary of State the registration by that name is intended to facilitate the commission of an offence involving dishonesty or deception` IS A BOOLEAN
      `in the opinion of the Secretary of State the registration by that name is intended to facilitate conduct that, if carried out in any part of the United Kingdom, would amount to such an offence` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 53A the company may be registered by the name` MEANS
    IF       `in the opinion of the Secretary of State the registration by that name is intended to facilitate the commission of an offence involving dishonesty or deception`
         OR  `in the opinion of the Secretary of State the registration by that name is intended to facilitate conduct that, if carried out in any part of the United Kingdom, would amount to such an offence`
    THEN JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "A company must not be registered under this Act by a name if, in the opinion of the Secretary of State, the registration of the company by that name is intended to facilitate—"
    ELSE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the prohibition names only the cases in (a) and (b)

